# Documentation Heading Numbering

Use hierarchical decimal numbering in all markdown docs under `docs/` and `specs/`.

## Format

| Markdown level | Example | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `#` (title) | `Architecture Reference` | Document title only — **no number** |
| `##` (section) | `## 1. High-Level Architecture` | Top-level sections: `{n}.` |
| `###` (subsection) | `### 1.1 Gateway` | `{n}.{m}` — no trailing period |
| `####` (subsubsection) | `#### 1.1.1 Responsibilities` | `{n}.{m}.{k}` — no trailing period |
| `#####` and deeper | `##### 1.1.1.1 runners` | Continue the pattern |

## Rules

1. Number every heading except the document title (`#`) and `## Table of Contents`.
2. Reset child counters when a parent section changes (e.g. after `### 1.2`, the next `####` is `#### 1.2.1`, not `#### 1.1.4`).
3. Strip and replace old numbers when renumbering — never stack numbers (`### 1.1 1.1 Gateway` is wrong).
4. Do not number lines inside fenced code blocks.
5. Keep anchor links in the TOC consistent with heading text (e.g. `### 1.1 Gateway` → `#11-gateway`).

## Example

```markdown
# Feature Name

## Table of Contents

1. [Overview](#1-overview)
   - [Goals](#11-goals)

---

## 1. Overview

### 1.1 Goals

#### 1.1.1 Primary goal

Content here.

### 1.2 Scope

#### 1.2.1 In scope
```
